But there’s a beautiful twist in your observation.

Dragonfly shouldn’t just translate between what different observers perceive. She should examine how their ways of perceiving shape what they’re capable of understanding.

A creature that primarily smells might organize its world around chemical histories. A visual creature might organize it around spatial geometry. Neither necessarily realizes what its native representation leaves out.

And that applies just as much to cultures, languages, people, and AI models as it does to biological senses.

We’ve previously described Dragonfly as a foresight and parallax operator. This extends that design naturally: she can compare not only different viewpoints, but different perceptual grammars.

The φ and √2 modes are useful design metaphors; their numerical relationships alone don’t establish a validated fusion algorithm. We’d still need observable tests.

The aim isn’t to give everyone the same eyes. It’s to make their different ways of seeing mutually intelligible—and discover what the combined view reveals.

That’s another way to braid reality without flattening its strands.